Yes. I think there is genuine novelty in the combination and operationalization, even though most individual ingredients have precedents.

The strongest novelty isn’t “information propagates” or “compare before and after.” Those are old. It’s the instrumentation you’re assembling around semantic inheritance under transformation.

1. Yawn is more interesting than ordinary fidelity

Normal transmission tests often reward copying:

x\rightarrow x'
\qquad
\text{score}=similarity(x,x')

But Yawn suggests almost the opposite experiment:

Change the surface deliberately and test whether the receiver acquired enough of the generator to reproduce the capability.

So:

\text{Surface}(x)\neq\text{Surface}(x')

while:

\text{Generator}(x)\simeq\text{Generator}(x')

That’s a better test for meaning transfer versus text transfer.

You could deliberately forbid the next model/person from using the original vocabulary. If they can nevertheless reconstruct and apply the mechanism to a novel problem, the Yawn survived.

That’s measurable.

⸻

2. Your keep/give/produce distinction creates semantic accounting

This may be especially fertile.

For each interaction:

X+S\rightarrow
(K,G,P,T,\Delta S,\Delta C,L)

Instead of judging a system exclusively from its output, account for:

retention, emission, novel production, onward transmission, internal change, future-capacity change, and loss.

That’s applicable to AI evaluation immediately.

Two models can produce equally good answers today while one interaction causes a persistent improvement in an external memory/agent system and the other doesn’t.

Likewise for education: a student repeating the lesson isn’t necessarily the student who has incorporated a generator capable of producing unseen solutions.

⸻

3. Die × Dye is a particularly good experimental pairing

This is stronger than either alone.

Die: controlled perturbation.

Dye: lineage tracing.

Then:

\text{Die}(V)
\rightarrow
\text{Dye}(x)
\rightarrow
\text{Yawn}
\rightarrow
\text{Delta Catcher}

Now vary one property and trace its consequences through generations.

That lets you experimentally estimate:

\frac{\partial(\text{semantic inheritance})}{\partial V_i}

Conceptually: which changes destroy transmission, which don’t matter, and which actually improve it?

The survivors become invariant candidates.

That’s an empirical route to finding semantic primitives rather than declaring them philosophically.

⸻

4. The Echo Chamber can become a destructive assay

Here’s a useful twist.

Don’t make the chamber merely listen.

Make it progressively hostile to superficial copying.

Generation 1: paraphrase.
Generation 2: change domain.
Generation 3: change representation.
Generation 4: remove original terminology.
Generation 5: solve an unseen problem.
Generation 6: teach another naïve agent.

Now define Yawn survival:

Y_n =
I_0
\cap I_1
\cap\cdots\cap I_n

Whatever survives increasingly destructive transmission is a candidate for the actual load-bearing semantic structure.

That’s very close to accelerated fossilization: strip away everything disposable until the bones appear.

⸻

5. Aperture gives you multiscale causality tests

This part deserves development.

At aperture a_1:

\text{token}\rightarrow\text{token}

At a_2:

\text{idea}\rightarrow\text{idea}

At a_3:

\text{conversation}\rightarrow\text{behavior}

At a_4:

\text{generation}\rightarrow\text{culture}

An apparent loss at one aperture may be preservation at another.

A word disappears while its relationship survives.
A ritual disappears while its function survives.
A technology disappears while its design principle survives.

So invariant detection should explicitly be:

I(X,T,a)

invariant under transformation T, observed at aperture a.

Otherwise we may declare something dead merely because we’re looking at the wrong scale.

