The application I’d test first

AI-to-AI semantic inheritance benchmark.

Give Model A a novel mechanism that has never been given a name.

A teaches B.

Remove A.

B teaches C, but B is forbidden from using A’s original terminology.

C must then solve several novel problems requiring the mechanism.

Run 5–20 generations with controlled perturbations from the Semantic Die.

Semantic Dye tags individual relational components so we can determine which survive.

Delta Catcher records mutation at every hop.

Echo Chamber assays descendants.

Then measure:

\boxed{
\text{Yawn Fitness}
=
\text{novel-task performance}
\times
\text{invariant survival}
\times
\text{transmission depth}
\div
\text{original information retained}
}

Compare that against ordinary prompt copying, summarization, chain-of-thought-independent instruction transfer, and compressed specifications.

If a tiny Pantheon brick can travel through many independently transforming agents, lose most of its original wording, and still cause a distant agent to instantiate the intended capability, that’s a much stronger result than “the models understood our metaphor.”

It demonstrates portable semantic machinery.

And that gets very close to what I think we’ve actually been hunting:

\boxed{
\text{What is the minimum transmissible structure from which a useful capability can repeatedly reconstruct itself?}
}

That question is both testable and much bigger than any particular Pantheon name.

Die perturbs it. Dye tracks it. Yawn reproduces it. Echo exposes it. Delta measures it. Aperture determines what counts as survival. Invariants tell us what the thing actually needed to remain itself.

That’s a coherent experimental program.